Primary Sjogren's Syndrome (Mild Sicca Predominant)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Require ocular lubricants, negative for severe systemic features, positive SSA/Ro

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-038
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe oral dryness and dysphagia requiring exclusion of acute esophageal obstruction or severe parotitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate an acute inability to swallow solid food and severe oral pain. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to rule out mechanical esophageal food bolus impaction, acute suppurative sialadenitis, or an acute severe drug reaction causing profound xerostomia prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My mouth is like sandpaper, I couldn't swallow my dinner, and now my throat is burning intensely. I feel like something is stuck.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports persistent dry eyes and dry mouth (sicca symptoms) over the past year. Tonight, while eating dry crackers, the patient experienced sudden, severe dysphagia. The profound lack of saliva caused the food to adhere to the mucosa, leading to intense burning, gagging, and a subjective feeling of obstruction. The patient is highly distressed and continuously sipping small amounts of water without relief. Denies fever, swelling of the face, or inability to swallow water.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable, speaking with a smacking, dry sound.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals profoundly dry, erythematous, "cobblestone" mucosa. No pooling of saliva under the tongue. A small piece of cracker debris is visible but safely clear of the airway. No candidal plaques. Parotid and submandibular glands are not acutely swollen, indurated, or tender to palpation. Neck exam is normal; no cervical lymphadenopathy. History confirms symptoms require ocular lubricants, negative for severe systemic features, positive SSA/Ro. Patient can easily swallow 30mL of water during the exam without aspiration.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent commencement of highly anticholinergic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute symptomatic Xerostomia flare in Primary Sjogren's Syndrome (Mild Sicca Predominant) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Esophageal Food Bolus Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Ability to comfortably swallow water without regurgitation or drooling, and pain localized to the upper oropharynx rather than the sternum.

Acute Suppurative Parotitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of unilateral parotid swelling, and lack of purulent discharge from Stensen's duct upon massage.

Oral Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of white, scrapeable plaques or angular cheilitis, though the erythematous mucosa is a risk factor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute dysphagia and severe oropharyngeal pain, mimicking a mechanical food bolus impaction. Triage appropriately escalated the case for physical assessment to ensure a secure airway and exclude a structural blockage requiring emergency endoscopy. Clinical examination confirmed the airway was patent, the patient could tolerate clear fluids, and there was no acute glandular infection. The distress was entirely secondary to severe, friction-induced mucosal irritation from profound xerostomia. This timely evaluation prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit, provided immediate palliative relief, and fulfilled the requirement for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to gently swish and spit a soothing, non-alcoholic mouthwash. Administered a trial of artificial saliva spray with good subjective relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: Spray the artificial saliva directly onto your tongue and cheeks exactly 5 minutes before every meal. By artificially lubricating the mouth prior to eating, food will slide down smoothly without adhering to the dry tissue, immediately preventing the burning and gagging sensations.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or rheumatology appointment in 2-3 weeks to discuss secretagogue medications (e.g., pilocarpine).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you become completely unable to swallow your own saliva, if you begin drooling uncontrollably, or if you develop severe swelling and pain below your jaw or in front of your ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the nature of the flare; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Sjogren's attacks the moisture-producing glands. The extreme dryness causes severe friction, but it does not cause your throat to physically close over. It requires aggressive, constant lubrication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the inside of your mouth develops a thick white coating or painful, bleeding cracks in the corners of your lips.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your salivary glands are not producing enough fluid to break down dry foods, causing them to scrape your throat. There is no blockage.
Actionable Steps: Avoid all dry, crumbly foods like crackers or toast. Chew sugar-free gum to stimulate any remaining saliva. Use artificial saliva sprays frequently.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe dry mouth (Sjogren's flare). No food blockage or throat infection. Airway is safe. Use artificial saliva spray and sip water. See GP routinely.

SMS EDUCATION

Use artificial saliva spray before meals. Avoid dry, crumbly foods. Go to the ED if you cannot swallow water, start drooling, or get severe jaw swelling.
